{# ─── Act 1 — Launch (Earth → L2) ────────────────────────────────── #}
{# Tall runway with a sticky inner stage so the Earth zoom-out can be
   scroll-scrubbed via getSectionProgress(). The 3D scene renders into
   #launch-scene; the 2D <canvas id="starfield"> is the reduced-motion /
   no-WebGL fallback, kept behind everything. #}
<section id="launch" class="hero-section hero-section-tall" data-rail-target="launch">
  <div class="hero-stage">
    <canvas id="starfield" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
    <div id="launch-scene" class="launch-scene" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="scene-fallback"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-vignette" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="hero-text">
      <p class="hero-kicker">01 · Launch</p>
      <h1 class="hero-name">{{ profile.name }}</h1>
      <p class="hero-title">{{ profile.title }}</p>
      <p class="hero-affiliation">
        <a href="{{ profile.affiliation_url }}" class="hero-affil-link"
           target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ profile.affiliation_full }}</a>
      </p>

      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="hero-btn" href="#skysphere">Begin the story</a>
        <a class="hero-btn outline" href="cv/LucasSauniere_CV.pdf">Download CV</a>
      </div>

      <ul class="hero-socials">
        <li><a href="{{ profile.links.github }}"   aria-label="GitHub">GitHub</a></li>
        <li><a href="{{ profile.links.scholar }}"  aria-label="Google Scholar">Scholar</a></li>
        <li><a href="{{ profile.links.orcid }}"    aria-label="ORCID">ORCID</a></li>
        <li><a href="{{ profile.links.linkedin }}" aria-label="LinkedIn">LinkedIn</a></li>
        <li><a href="mailto:{{ profile.email }}"   aria-label="Email">Email</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="scroll-hint" aria-hidden="true">Scroll to leave Earth</div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ─── Act 2 — Sky coverage (mirror sphere) ───────────────────────── #}
<section id="skysphere" class="scroll-section" data-rail-target="skysphere">
  <div class="scroll-section-inner">
    <div class="scroll-section-text">
      <p class="act-kicker">02 · Sky coverage</p>
      <h2>A third of the sky, year by year.</h2>
      <p>
        From the gravitational calm of Lagrange point 2 — 1.5 million
        kilometres from Earth — ESA's <em>Euclid</em> space telescope sweeps
        out a survey of roughly <strong>1.5 billion galaxies</strong> across
        a third of the entire sky, over six years.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here the survey footprint paints itself onto the celestial sphere as
        a white leading edge, one mission year at a time, over the Milky Way.
        Its goal: to chart the geometry of the dark Universe.
      </p>
      <p class="scroll-hint">Scroll: the footprint advances, year by year.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll-section-visual scroll-section-visual-wide">
      <div id="skysphere-scene" class="scene-container scene-skysphere" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="scene-fallback">Initialising survey…</div>
        <div class="skysphere-year" aria-hidden="true">Year 1</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scene-caption">
        Euclid six-year survey footprint, accumulating over the Milky Way and
        reflected in the celestial sphere.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ─── Act 3 — From photon to PSF ──────────────────────────────── #}
<section id="photon" class="scroll-section" data-rail-target="photon">
  <div class="scroll-section-inner">
    <div class="scroll-section-text">
      <p class="act-kicker">03 · Optics</p>
      <h2>From photon to PSF.</h2>
      <p>
        A star is, for all practical purposes, a point. But no telescope
        records a point. Light passes through a circular aperture and
        diffracts; whatever aberrations live in the optics imprint
        themselves on the wavefront; what lands on the detector is a
        smeared-out fingerprint called the <em>point spread function</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every source in every image is this shape, scaled by brightness.
        Recover the PSF and you recover the truth behind the blur.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll-section-visual">
      <div id="photon-scene" class="scene-container scene-wide" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="scene-fallback">Computing diffraction…</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scene-caption">
        Plane wave → pupil with wavefront phase → focal-plane PSF.
        Computed live via FFT of the aperture field.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ─── Act 4 — The problem ────────────────────────────────────── #}
<section id="problem" class="scroll-section" data-rail-target="problem">
  <div class="scroll-section-inner">
    <div class="scroll-section-text">
      <p class="act-kicker">04 · The problem</p>
      <h2>An ideal telescope doesn't exist.</h2>
      <p>
        If Euclid were diffraction-limited, the PSF would be a crisp Airy
        pattern — the mathematical ideal. In practice, thermal gradients
        flex the optical bench, the primary mirror settles by nanometres
        after launch, and the spacecraft itself breathes as it orbits the
        Sun. Each effect smears the PSF in a different direction.
      </p>
      <p>
        A millimetre of astigmatism at the mirror becomes a galaxy-scale
        ellipticity bias in the final catalogue — exactly the signal we're
        trying to measure. So we measure the blur instead.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll-section-visual">
      <div id="problem-scene" class="scene-container scene-wide" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="scene-fallback">Preparing comparison…</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scene-caption">
        Left: diffraction-limited. Right: as-built, with realistic low-order aberrations.
        RMS wavefront error scales with scroll progress.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ─── Act 5 — Imaging (why the PSF matters) ──────────────────── #}
<section id="imaging" class="scroll-section" data-rail-target="imaging">
  <div class="scroll-section-inner">
    <div class="scroll-section-text">
      <p class="act-kicker">05 · Imaging</p>
      <h2>Why we care.</h2>
      <p>
        Euclid's headline science is <em>weak gravitational lensing</em>:
        measuring the tiny ellipticities of distant galaxies to map the
        dark Universe. The cosmological signal is roughly a one-percent
        shear on each galaxy. The telescope's aberrated PSF imprints a
        few-percent shape bias of its own — same units, larger amplitude,
        wrong physics.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the left, a synthetic patch of sky as it really is. On the
        right, the same patch after the as-built PSF has done its work:
        the field convolved with the kernel in the middle. Round
        ellipticals lean the way the PSF leans; small galaxies get
        merged; point sources blur into the kernel itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        That bias has to be subtracted before any cosmology is done. To
        subtract it, we have to know the PSF — at every position on the
        focal plane, at every wavelength, at every time. The rest of the
        story is how.
      </p>
      <p class="scroll-hint">Scroll: aberration ramps up; the field smears.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll-section-visual scroll-section-visual-wide">
      <div id="imaging-scene" class="scene-container scene-wide" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="scene-fallback">Convolving field…</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scene-caption">
        True scene → as-built PSF (defocus + astigmatism + coma) → observed scene.
        Aberration RMS ramps with scroll, in waves of the operating wavelength.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ─── Act 6 — Measurement ─────────────────────────────────── #}
<section id="measurement" class="scroll-section" data-rail-target="measurement">
  <div class="scroll-section-inner">
    <div class="scroll-section-text">
      <p class="act-kicker">06 · Measurement</p>
      <h2>The stars tell us the PSF.</h2>
      <p>
        Every exposure contains tens of thousands of stars. Stars are
        unresolved — their image <em>is</em> the PSF at that field
        position, modulo photon noise. By collecting stars across the
        focal plane we build a sparse but global map of the blur.
      </p>
      <p>
        But the PSF varies: with position on the 6×6 CCD mosaic, with
        wavelength, with time, with the spacecraft's thermal state.
        No two stars share the same PSF exactly. The map has to
        interpolate, extrapolate, and denoise — all at once.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll-section-visual">
      <div id="measurement-scene" class="scene-container scene-wide" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="scene-fallback">Measuring PSFs…</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scene-caption">
        Full VIS focal plane → single CCD → single stellar PSF.
        Stars drawn from a realistic luminosity distribution.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ─── Act 7 — Denoise (DDPM) ───────────────────────────────── #}
<section id="denoise" class="scroll-section scroll-section-tall" data-rail-target="denoise">
  <div class="scroll-section-inner">
    <div class="scroll-section-text">
      <p class="act-kicker">07 · Denoise</p>
      <h2>The raw stamps are awful.</h2>
      <p>
        The stars we use as PSF probes don't cooperate: most are faint,
        so the stamps are dominated by shot noise; and VIS samples the
        PSF below Nyquist, so even at high signal-to-noise the stamp on
        the detector is a coarse, pixelated rendering of the true
        continuous PSF. A polynomial fit to those stamps inherits both
        problems and bakes them into the model.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our pipeline sidesteps that by using a <em>denoising diffusion
        probabilistic model</em> (DDPM) as a learned prior over PSFs.
        Given a noisy, undersampled stamp, the DDPM runs the reverse
        diffusion process for T steps, peeling off variance at each
        step, until what remains is a clean PSF estimate consistent
        with everything the network has ever seen during training. Only
        then does the regressor see the stamp.
      </p>
      <p class="scroll-hint">Scroll: noise comes off in T steps.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll-section-visual scroll-section-visual-wide">
      <div id="denoise-scene" class="scene-container scene-tall" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="scene-fallback">Loading DDPM trajectory…</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scene-caption">
        Raw stamp (T = 9) → DDPM reverse trajectory → clean stamp (T = 0)
        → feeds the Inception regressor in the next Act.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ─── Act 8 — The Network ─────────────────────────────────── #}
<section id="network" class="scroll-section scroll-section-tall" data-rail-target="network">
  <div class="scroll-section-inner">
    <div class="scroll-section-text">
      <p class="act-kicker">08 · The Network</p>
      <h2>From PSF stamp to Zernike coefficients.</h2>
      <p>
        Once the PSF is denoised, an Inception-style CNN regresses the
        Zernike coefficients that describe its wavefront. The first layer
        does the heavy lifting: a small kernel is dragged across the
        stamp, and at every position it sums the input pixels weighted
        by the kernel. The output is a feature map that responds where
        the input matches what the kernel is tuned to detect.
      </p>
      <p>
        An <em>Inception block</em> runs several kernel sizes in parallel
        — 1×1, 3×3, 5×5 — and concatenates their feature maps, so the
        network can pick up both fine pixel-level structure and broader
        shape cues in the same forward pass. Stack a few of those blocks
        and a regression head, and the network maps a 32-pixel stamp to
        a handful of Zernike coefficients: defocus (Z₄), astigmatism
        (Z₅, Z₆), coma (Z₇, Z₈), spherical (Z₁₁).
      </p>
      <p class="scroll-hint">Scroll: convolution slides, then the bars fill in.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll-section-visual scroll-section-visual-wide">
      <div id="network-scene" class="scene-container scene-tall" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="scene-fallback">Loading network…</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scene-caption">
        Aberrated PSF → 5×5 convolution sliding across the stamp → Inception
        block (1×1, 3×3, 5×5 in parallel) → predicted Zernike coefficients.
        White ticks mark the ground-truth values.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ─── Act 9 — Validation ──────────────────────────────────── #}
<section id="validation" class="scroll-section scroll-section-tall" data-rail-target="validation">
  <div class="scroll-section-inner">
    <div class="scroll-section-text">
      <p class="act-kicker">09 · Validation</p>
      <h2>Does it work?</h2>
      <p>
        An unvalidated model is a rumour. To trust the network we need to
        see what it predicts on stars it has <em>never seen</em>, compare
        those predictions to truth, and check that the residuals are
        consistent with noise — not only on average, but across every
        corner of the focal plane.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the right: three panels for a held-out star — truth, prediction,
        and residual. As training proceeds, the residual collapses toward
        the photon-noise floor. The map below shows fractional size error
        ΔT / T across the field. We want it featureless.
      </p>
      <p class="scroll-hint">Scroll: watch training converge.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll-section-visual scroll-section-visual-wide">
      <div id="validation-scene" class="scene-container scene-validation" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="scene-fallback">Loading validation…</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scene-caption">
        Held-out star, residual to noise floor, and focal-plane size error.
        Residual colour-stretched ±5% of peak.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ─── Act 10 — Euclid M2 focus ramps ────────────────────────── #}
<section id="focus-ramp" class="scroll-section scroll-section-tall" data-rail-target="focus-ramp">
  <div class="scroll-section-inner">
    <div class="scroll-section-text">
      <p class="act-kicker">10 · Euclid</p>
      <h2>Two ramps, one minimum.</h2>
      <p>
        Commissioning used two M2 focus ramps. A <strong>coarse</strong>
        ramp walked Δz<sub>M2</sub> from −50 to +50 µm in steps
        of 10 µm, three exposures per step — wide enough to bracket the
        true best focus. A <strong>fine</strong> ramp then refined it
        from −10 to +10 µm in steps of 2 µm.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because VIS undersamples the PSF, the model recovers only
        |Z₄|: positive and negative defocus leave
        near-identical stamps after pixelation. The scatter is
        therefore a V, not a line. Best focus sits at the vertex.
      </p>
      <p>
        As you scroll, the coarse ramp builds its V; then the fine
        ramp fills in, tightening the vertex. The recovered best focus
        is reported against the engineering value both in absolute
        microns and normalised by the campaign's step size.
      </p>
      <p class="scroll-hint">Scroll: coarse ramp, then fine ramp.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll-section-visual scroll-section-visual-wide">
      <div id="focusramp-scene" class="scene-container scene-focusramp" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="scene-fallback">Loading focus ramps…</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scene-caption">
        Left: stacked PSF at current Δz<sub>M2</sub>. Right: extracted |Z₄| vs
        Δz<sub>M2</sub>, V-shape fit, recovered vertex against engineering best focus.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

